captain. Phileas Fogg, with body straight and legs apart, stood like a sailor at the bow of the

10
00:00:55.662 --> 00:00:59.174
boat. He gazed at the swelling waters without

11
00:00:59.175 --> 00:01:03.630
staggering. Aouda, who sat further back, looked out upon the

12
00:01:03.631 --> 00:01:08.833
ocean. The boat, carried forward by the wind, seemed to be flying over the

13
00:01:08.834 --> 00:01:12.876
waves. Fix, seated below, kept apart from his fellow

14
00:01:12.877 --> 00:01:17.464
travelers. He did not like to talk to the man whose favors he had been forced to

15
00:01:17.465 --> 00:01:22.482
accept. "Once in the United States, what should I do?" he asked

16
00:01:22.483 --> 00:01:30.468
himself. "Should I abandon my man? No! A hundred times, no! I will not lose sight of him for an

17
00:01:30.469 --> 00:01:34.903
hour!" Mr. Fogg and Aouda ate with good appetites that

18
00:01:34.904 --> 00:01:39.207
evening. Fix was invited to share their meal, which he accepted

19
00:01:39.208 --> 00:01:43.854
reluctantly. To travel at this man's expense and live upon his provisions was distasteful to

20
00:01:43.855 --> 00:01:47.186
him. Still, he was obliged to eat so he

21
00:01:47.187 --> 00:01:54.776
ate. When the meal was over, he took Mr. Fogg aside and said, "Sir, you have been very kind to pay my way on this

22
00:01:54.777 --> 00:01:57.642
boat. But . . . I must ask to pay my

23
00:01:57.643 --> 00:02:01.099
share." "Let us not speak of that, sir," replied Mr.

24
00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:04.649
Fogg. Fix was determined. "But, I

25
00:02:04.650 --> 00:02:10.619
insist—" "No, sir," repeated Mr. Fogg. "I consider it part of my general expenses."

26
00:02:10.620 --> 00:02:15.985
Fix bowed, returned to his cot, and did not speak again that evening.

27
00:02:15.986 --> 00:02:20.334
At daybreak the next morning, the wind began to blow harder.

28
00:02:20.335 --> 00:02:24.163
The sun had set in a red mist the night before.

29
00:02:24.164 --> 00:02:28.399
John Bunsby had studied ocean weather long enough to know what it meant.

30
00:02:28.400 --> 00:02:32.654
"We are going to have a rough wind," the captain said to Mr. Fogg.

31
00:02:32.655 --> 00:02:36.468
"Is the wind north or south?" asked Mr. Fogg.

32
00:02:36.469 --> 00:02:41.351
"South. Look! A typhoon is coming up!" said the captain.

33
00:02:41.352 --> 00:02:47.551
"I am glad it's a typhoon from the south, as it will carry us forward!" said Mr. Fogg.

34
00:02:47.552 --> 00:02:51.138
The captain took precautions and tied up all the sails.

35
00:02:51.139 --> 00:02:55.586
The top masts were put away, and all hands went forward.

36
00:02:55.587 --> 00:03:06.037
A single triangular sail of strong canvas was hoisted at the bow to keep the boat from turning into the wind. Then they waited.

37
00:03:06.038 --> 00:03:13.572
John Bunsby had requested that all passengers go below, but Mr. Fogg, Aouda, and Fix chose to remain on deck.

38
00:03:13.573 --> 00:03:20.404
None of them wanted to feel imprisoned in a narrow space with very little air while the boat bounced about.

39
00:03:20.405 --> 00:03:24.328
The storm and rain started around eight o'clock.

40
00:03:24.329 --> 00:03:31.658
The Tankadere was lifted like a feather by a violent wind.

41
00:03:31.659 --> 00:03:35.350
The boat flew northward, carried by monstrous waves.

42
00:03:35.351 --> 00:03:43.919
The Tankadere was almost submerged numerous times by mountains of water that rose behind it, but the skill of the captain saved the boat. The

43
00:03:43.920 --> 00:03:50.306
passengers were often bathed in a cold spray that was whipped by the wind. Fix cursed it. Aouda

44
00:03:50.307 --> 00:03:55.092
kept her eyes fastened on her protector, whose coolness amazed her. As

45
00:03:55.093 --> 00:04:00.639
for Mr. Fogg, he acted as if the typhoon was just part of his plan. The

46
00:04:00.640 --> 00:04:04.833
Tankadere kept steady on its northward track. The

47
00:04:04.834 --> 00:04:10.946
night was a terror, and it was a miracle that the boat did not sink. Twice

48
00:04:10.947 --> 00:04:15.738
the boat would have gone down if the crew had not been constantly on the watch. Aouda

49
00:04:15.739 --> 00:04:22.988
was exhausted, and more than once, Mr. Fogg rushed to protect her from the violence of the waves. Day

50
00:04:22.989 --> 00:04:27.665
reappeared and the tempest raged just as fiercely. By

51
00:04:27.666 --> 00:04:33.091
noon there were signs that the wind was calming, and the night was comparatively quiet. Some

52
00:04:33.092 --> 00:04:37.577
of the sails were raised, and the Tankadere moved forward with speed again. The

53
00:04:37.578 --> 00:04:43.807
next morning at dawn, the passengers could see the coast of Shanghai. The

54
00:04:43.808 --> 00:04:46.733
wind and waves had grown much calmer. At

55
00:04:46.734 --> 00:04:51.574
seven, they were still three miles from Shanghai when a long black smokestack, crowned

56
00:04:51.575 --> 00:04:56.426
with a plume of white smoke, appeared on the waters near the coast. "Blast

57
00:04:56.427 --> 00:05:06.414
our luck!" cried John Bunsby. "The Yokohama steamer has left the port!" Passepartout's

58
00:05:06.415 --> 00:05:10.153
Guidebook I heard that was quite a rough storm Mr. Fogg experienced!

59
00:05:10.154 --> 00:05:15.167
I'm glad I missed that leg of the journey. I would have gotten terribly seasick.

60
00:05:15.168 --> 00:05:19.598
Imagine going through all that and then missing the Yokohama steamer.

61
00:05:19.599 --> 00:05:24.608
Yokohama was a small fishing village from the eighth century until the nineteenth century.

62
00:05:24.609 --> 00:05:30.848
In 1854 the Americans arrived with warships and demanded that Japan allow international trade.

63
00:05:30.849 --> 00:05:36.022
In response, the Japanese made Yokohama a port where foreign ships could dock.

64
00:05:36.023 --> 00:05:43.857
Raw silk, tea, and many other products were traded through Yokohama, and this little town was transformed into a city.

65
00:05:43.858 --> 00:05:54.862
Many Western influences first reached Japan through Yokohama, including the country's first daily newspaper and first railway.

